@meta
id: 25-16
title: Every buffer, every window, every tab
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: bufdo, argdo, ex-normal
par: 63
minutes: 8
vscode: Find in Files, Replace All, hope the fixture did not match
open: order.provider.ts refund.provider.ts payment.service.ts payments.fixture.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
The gateway client is being renamed. Every use of the identifier
`legacyGateway` becomes `paymentGateway`, in all three source files.

The fourth buffer, `payments.fixture.ts`, holds recorded gateway
responses. It contains the *string* `'legacy-gateway'`, which is what the
old provider actually sent on the wire and is not being renamed. Touch it
and the check fails.

A range addresses lines in one buffer. `:bufdo` is the next size up: it
runs an Ex command in every buffer on the buffer list, one after another.
`:%s/…/…/g` is an Ex command. So is `:normal`. So is a `|`-separated pair
of them, which is how the write gets done: `| update` writes each buffer
before moving to the next, and only if that buffer actually changed.

@keys
| `:bufdo %s/old/new/ge | update` | substitute in every buffer, write the ones that changed |
| `:argdo` | the same, over the argument list |
| `:windo` `:tabdo` | over every window in this tab, or every tab |
| `:update` | write, but only if the buffer is modified |
| `/ge` | `g` every match on the line, `e` do not stop on buffers with no match |

@hints
One command, in front of a substitute you already know how to write.
The identifier is a whole word and the fixture's string is hyphenated, so
a plain `legacyGateway` matches nothing in the fixture.
--
Without the `e` flag the run stops at the first buffer that has no match,
and half the job is done. Without `| update` nothing is written at all.
--
`:bufdo %s/legacyGateway/paymentGateway/ge | update` Enter, then `:qa`.

@solution
    :bufdo %s/legacyGateway/paymentGateway/ge | update<Enter>  :qa<Enter>

Fifty-five keystrokes for three files, and the same command for thirty.

Four commands, one shape. They differ only in which list they walk:

| `:argdo` | the argument list — the files you named on the command line, or set with `:args` |
| `:bufdo` | the buffer list — everything vim has loaded, including files you opened to glance at |
| `:windo` | the windows in the current tab |
| `:tabdo` | the tabs |

And from chapter 19, `:cdo` and `:cfdo` walk the quickfix list. Pick by
asking which list already contains exactly the files you mean. `:bufdo`
is the loose one: a buffer you opened an hour ago to check something is
still on the list, and `:bufdo %s/…/g` will edit it. `:argdo` after
`:args src/**/*.ts` is the precise one, because you said what the list
was.

Three details that bite:

**`| update`, not `| w`.** `update` writes only modified buffers, so the
timestamps of untouched files do not change and your build does not
rebuild the world. Here it is also what keeps the fixture honest.

**The `e` flag.** `%s` with no match raises `E486`, and an error inside
`:bufdo` stops the whole run part-way. `/ge` says "carry on".

**Where you end up.** The commands leave you in the last buffer of the
list, not the one you started in. `` `` `` or `Ctrl-O` gets you back.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { legacyGateway } from '../../infrastructure/gateways';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  public async authorise(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return legacyGateway.authorise(orderId, amount);
  }

  public async capture(orderId: string) {
    return legacyGateway.capture(orderId);
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { paymentGateway } from '../../infrastructure/gateways';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  public async authorise(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return paymentGateway.authorise(orderId, amount);
  }

  public async capture(orderId: string) {
    return paymentGateway.capture(orderId);
  }
}

@start refund.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { legacyGateway } from '../../infrastructure/gateways';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    const receipt = await legacyGateway.refund(orderId, amount);

    return receipt;
  }
}

@expected refund.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { paymentGateway } from '../../infrastructure/gateways';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    const receipt = await paymentGateway.refund(orderId, amount);

    return receipt;
  }
}

@start payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { legacyGateway } from '../gateways';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async status() {
    return legacyGateway.status();
  }

  public async settle(paymentId: string) {
    return legacyGateway.settle(paymentId);
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { paymentGateway } from '../gateways';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async status() {
    return paymentGateway.status();
  }

  public async settle(paymentId: string) {
    return paymentGateway.settle(paymentId);
  }
}

@start payments.fixture.ts
export const gatewayResponses = [
  { provider: 'legacy-gateway', status: 'AUTHORISED', reference: 'pay_9001' },
